Stacktrace and sample xml attached.

When renaming a node (i.e. keeping the same parent) using Session.move(srcPath, destPath), where the parent node doesn't allow same name siblings, the following exception is thrown:

{code:java}
Caused by: javax.jcr.ItemExistsException: A node definition that allows same name siblings could not be found for the node ...
{code}

This seems to be caused by the following line in JcrSession.move:

{code:java}
destParentNode.validateChildNodeDefinition(srcNode.name(), srcNode.getPrimaryTypeName(), true);
{code}

I think it should read the following instead:

{code:java}
destParentNode.validateChildNodeDefinition(destPath.getLastSegment().getName(), srcNode.getPrimaryTypeName(), true);
{code}

Create a multi-value property of reference type, then doing the following will give truncated node identifiers:

{code:java}
Property prop = node.getProperty("propname");
Value[] values = prop.getValues();

for (Value value : values) {
   String referencedId = value.getString(); // the ID is truncated!
   Node node = session.getNodeByIdentifier(referenceId); // throws ItemNotFoundException
}
{code}

After some brief investigation it seems that the fault might be in DocumentTranslator.createReferenceFromString where valueStr only contains the node identifier with no source or workspace keys. This is passed to the NodeKey(String) constructor, where it expects a key but instead receives an identifier.

When NodeKey.getIdentifier() is called later, the identifier is truncated, as the NodeKey was not constructed correctly.

